here  are business people there are  communities who are coming together to  build and the next Labor government is  going to hand them tools to do that  whether it's Powers over skills  transport job centers housing these are  the things that enable places to thrive  and enable this country to succeed again  and we're going to hand those tools back  to communities as a partnership between  government and the people so that we can  build the country that we know that we  can be  I was interested to hear sakir talking  about taking back control the fact that  when he was uh talking to people around  the country about remaining uh on the  leave vote on the brexit vote that there  was a lot that he couldn't argue against  and so he appreciates that uh he's  trying to clearly appeal to both sides  levers and remainers and once again I  ask you is that possible it is possible  this country needs to move forwards we  found multiple ways to divide ourselves  from each other over the last few years  whether it's arguments about  independence in Scotland and growing  nationalism across England whether it's  the brexit divisions that we've been  scarred by for so long but actually when  we come together we build a country that  works and I think the the prevailing  mood of the times is that people want  this country to move forward we want to  start to rebuild our Public Services get  staff back into the National Health  Service get are buses and our on rail  networks working again and get people a  decent pay rise and all of these things  are only possible if we take a long-term  approach we make promises that we know  that we can deliver and don't make  promises that we can't and we involve  all people in all parts of Britain in  helping to rebuild our country  I'd like to ask you about these strikes  at the moment it's being called the Tory  party's winter of discontent which is  you know planning to present some laws  that ban strikes do you support striking  action I absolutely support the right to  strike but I desperately wish this  wasn't happening and so do the nurses  the postal workers the um the paramedics  all of the people at this country at the  moment that are struggling to keep their  Public Services going we need the  government to sit down and get round the  table and start to treat our Frontline  workers as partners in our national  Endeavor not as enemies and these laws  that the government are proposing are  the last Act of an absolutely desperate  government who understand the price of  everything but the value of nothing our  Frontline workers are going to help us  build our way out of this crisis and the  government needs to sit down with them  treat them as partners and work out a  plan  okay Lisa and Andy Shadow leveling up  secretary for housing and communities as  well thanks very much indeed thank you
 

